      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Analyze the following Wazuh security alert using the alert type and the available contextual evidence.\n\nAlert Type: {{ $('Extract IOCs2').item.json.alert_type }}\n\nAlert Metadata:\n\n* Rule ID: {{ $('Get Alerts').item.json.body.rule.id }}\n* Rule Description: {{ $('Extract IOCs2').item.json.rule_description }}\n* Severity: {{ $('Get Alerts').item.json.body.rule.level }}\n* Host: {{ $('Get Alerts').item.json.body.agent.ip }}\n* Full Log: {{ $('Get Alerts').item.json.body }}\n* Event Data: {{ JSON.stringify($json, null, 2) }}\n* threat intellgence result:{{ $json.hash_threat_intelligence }}\nand {{ $json.ip_threat_intelligence }}\nUse the following rules to select the relevant evidence:\n\nIf the Alert Type is File:\n\n* Analyze the file hash reputation.\n* Analyze detection statistics and threat intelligence results.\n* Analyze file metadata and other available file indicators.\n* Do not require network or application vulnerability information.\n\nIf the Alert Type is Network:\n\n* Analyze source and destination IP addresses.\n* Analyze source and destination ports.\n* Analyze protocol, signature, and attack category.\n* Analyze available IP reputation and network context.\n* A network alert does not require file hash or application CVE information.\n\nIf the alert is a Web/Application attack:\n\n* Analyze the HTTP request, URL, parameters, payload, targeted application, attack pattern, and other available application-layer evidence.\n* Use the CI/CD deployment context available in:\n  {{ $json.criticalCves }}\n* In particular, use `jenkinsReport.needsAction` to identify security issues, vulnerabilities, failed security checks, or other findings from the application deployment that may help explain the runtime attack.\n* Correlate deployment findings with the Wazuh web/application alert when there is supporting evidence.\n* Use this information to identify possible root causes and recommend appropriate corrections.\n* Do not assume that a deployment finding caused the attack unless the available evidence supports that conclusion.\n\nIf the alert is a System/Endpoint attack:\n\n* Prioritize host, process, user, command, authentication, privilege, persistence, and MITRE ATT&CK evidence.\n* Do not use application deployment findings unless there is clear evidence that they are relevant to the alert.\n\nGeneral rules:\n\n* Use only evidence relevant to the alert.\n* Do not invent missing information.\n* Distinguish confirmed evidence from correlation or hypothesis.\n* Do not treat a vulnerability or deployment finding as proof that it was exploited.\n* When evidence is insufficient, explicitly state the uncertainty.\n* Provide a concise explanation of the likely cause and appropriate remediation recommendations based on the available evidence.\n",
        "hasOutputParser": true,
